## Service Descriptions Methodology

```json
{
    "services":[
        {
            "name": "",
            "Trigering Event": "Some event in the enviroment that causes the SuD to deliver the service",
            "Delivered Service": "The effect on the enviroment of delivering the service",
            "Assumptions": [
                "Assumptions made on the enviroment"
            ]
        }
    ]
}
```

### Guidelines

- "MUST" specify system services in an **implementation-independent** way
- "MUST" fully bound the functionality of the SuD
- "SHOULD NOT" give details about all possible behaviors and communications that take place during the excecution of a service
- "SHOULD NOT" state exactly what the pre and post conditions of a service are
- "SHOULD NOT" give details about the data formats exchanged and about user interfaces
- "MUST" be **value-oriented**; this is, they emphasize the utility of the service for the user


### Triggering Event Guidelines
- "MUST" occur in the enviroment

### Delivered Service Guidelines

- "MUST" be described in terms of what happens in the environment (an **efect**)
- "MUST" make clear that this **effect** is valuable.
- "MUST" mention a quality attribute



### Assumptions Guidelines

When selecting the assumptions for each service description, you must follow this guidelines:

- "MUST" check if the SuD can observe whether the assumption is satisfied. If so, drop the assumption and incorporate it into stimulus-response specification
- "MUST" check if the SuD itself can ensure that the assumption will be satisfied. If so, drop the assumption and add functionality to the system that ensures that the assumption is satisfied



- "MUST" ensure that without this assumption, the **effect** is not valuable.

- "MUST" determine if the requirement should be different when the assumption is false.

- "MUST" check if the SuD cannot observe whether the assumption is satisfied, and also the SuD cannot ensure that it is satisfied, then verify if we can at least be certain that the environment ensures that it is satisfied. If not, include the assumption.

- "MUST" verify if you can be certain that the assumption is satisfied now, and also check if you can be sure that it is satisfied in the future. If not, include the assumption.

